{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:14:11Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 42, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "e2e/.pytest_cache/.gitignore\ne2e/.pytest_cache/CACHEDIR.TAG\ne2e/.pytest_cache/README.md\ne2e/.pytest_cache/v/cache/lastfailed\ne2e/.pytest_cache/v/cache/nodeids\ne2e/__pycache__/conftest.cpython-311-pytest-9.1.1.pyc\ne2e/__pycache__/conftest.cpython-311.pyc\ne2e/__pycache__/test_tasks_e2e.cpython-311-pytest-9.1.1.pyc\ne2e/conftest.py\ne2e/test_tasks_e2e.py\n---\n# Design: Give tasks a priority\n\nSource: Taiga #4 spec text as provided in this dispatch (see note below).\n\n> **Note on `docs/spec.md`:** at the time of writing, `docs/spec.md` on disk\n> still holds the previous cycle's content (Taiga #3, \"Edit a task title\").\n> The product-manager's Taiga #4 spec was included directly in this\n> dispatch's \"What earlier stages produced\" section, pending the user's\n> confirmation to overwrite `docs/spec.md`/`docs/design.md` and to resolve\n> open question #1 (interleaved vs. full 3-tier sort). This design is built\n> against that inline text, under the same \"interleaved \u2014 only `high`\n> moves\" assumption the spec proceeds under. Recommend the orchestrator\n> confirm both points with the user and persist `docs/spec.md` before the\n> developer stage starts, so the developer isn't working from a stale file\n> on disk.\n\n## Summary of decisions\n\n- **One control does both jobs.** Rather than a read-only badge plus a\n  separate control to change priority (two elements fighting for space in\n  an already-tight row \u2014 see round 2's `Edit`/`Done`/`Delete` row), each\n  task gets a single native `<select>` \u2014 `priority-select` \u2014 that always\n  shows the current priority as its own visible text (\"Low\"/\"Normal\"/\n  \"High\") and lets the user change it in one action. This satisfies AC8\n  (visible at a glance) and the \"control to change priority without\n  entering the title-edit form\" requirement (spec open question 2) with no\n  new UI pattern beyond what the app already has (a styled `<button>`- and\n  `<input>`-adjacent form control).\n- **Color only on the two priorities that deviate from baseline.** `normal`\n  renders with the same neutral border/background every other control in\n  this app uses (`#ccd2d6` border, white fill) \u2014 it's the default, so it\n  shouldn't compete visually. `high` gets a solid warm-orange fill (new\n  accent, not a reuse of `.error`'s red \u2014 see \"Why not reuse the error\n  red\" below). `low` gets a light neutral-gray fill, visually recessive.\n  This means scanning the list, only `high` rows pull the eye, which is\n  literally the ask (\"Let me... see that at a glance\").\n- **Why not reuse the error red (`#a3271d`) for `high`:** round 2's design\n  established the rule \"one red = one meaning\" for this app \u2014 `#a3271d`\n  means \"something is wrong.\" Reusing it for \"high priority\" would make a\n  perfectly valid, successfully-saved task look like an error state.\n  Introducing one new accent (orange, a different hue from red) keeps that\n  semantic separation intact while still giving `high` a color no other\n  element in the app currently uses.\n- **Change applies immediately on select, no separate Save.** Matches this\n  app's two existing precedents for single-field, low-risk actions:\n  `TaskFilter`'s segmented control (click \u2192 immediately re-filters) and the\n  `Done` button (click \u2192 immediately marks complete). A priority change is\n  the same shape of action \u2014 one field, no risk of data loss, no reason to\n  gate it behind a confirm step the way title editing is (title editing\n  needs Save/Cancel because it's multi-character free text where a stray", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01SDJ1Y4QkqczCsSCjfQC3fJ", "session_id": "d09eb7f3-5b08-4bee-8f0c-9efa0bcf1ce8"}}
